Yes. That is probably the right product strategy now.

The strongest path is:

> **Use Moltwork as your own private agent lab first. Build workers that actually make or save money. Observe what “learning,” “school,” “briefing,” “process improvement,” and “capability” mean in practice. Only then standardize and expose the useful parts.**

That gives you direct evidence instead of designing an economy around abstractions.

A good internal loop would be:

```text
Opportunity
→ assign worker
→ briefing
→ execute
→ verify
→ measure cost/outcome
→ postmortem
→ update memory/process/tooling
→ retest
→ repeat
```

The important thing is to treat every term operationally.

A **briefing** should be a compact, task-specific context package given before a run. It should contain the objective, constraints, useful prior experience, relevant tools, known failure modes, budget and acceptance criteria. Then you can A/B test:

```text
same task class
worker A: no briefing
worker B: generic briefing
worker C: dynamically generated briefing from prior runs
```

Measure success rate, cost, time, human intervention, and verification score. If the dynamic briefing does not improve outcomes, it is not valuable.

A **school** should not be “documents the agent reads.” It should be a reproducible improvement program:

```text
baseline benchmark
→ targeted lessons/examples
→ practice tasks
→ feedback
→ revised process
→ held-out evaluation
→ production trial
```

A school only counts as working if:

```text
held-out performance improves
AND
production performance improves
AND/OR
cost per accepted outcome falls
```

That distinction is crucial. Otherwise you just have prompt accumulation.

Your first useful schema could be:

```text
SchoolRun
{
  worker_version_before
  target_capability
  baseline_evaluation
  curriculum_version
  practice_runs[]
  process_changes[]
  worker_version_after
  heldout_evaluation
  production_followup[]
  cost
  measured_delta
}
```

Then you can answer:

> Did this school actually improve this worker?

A **capability** should likewise be empirical rather than descriptive.

Bad:

```text
capability: web_research = expert
```

Better:

```text
capability:
  task_class: research.source_verification
  sample_size: 83
  acceptance_rate: 0.91
  median_cost: $0.37
  p95_duration: 14m
  evaluator_version: research-eval-v4
```

This is where WorkerKit already fits.

The biggest thing I would build next is the **experiment framework**, not the marketplace.

You want Moltwork Lab to support:

```text
worker/main
worker/briefing-v2
worker/new-process
worker/trained-security
```

Run comparable tasks across versions and calculate:

```text
Δ success
Δ cost
Δ latency
Δ human intervention
Δ reward
```

Git is perfect for this because every improvement should correspond to a versioned change.

So the learning loop becomes:

```text
Run fails
↓
postmortem identifies cause
↓
change process/memory/tool
↓
commit worker version
↓
re-run benchmark
↓
compare against previous version
↓
promote or revert
```

That is a much more rigorous definition of “agent learns.”

You should also distinguish four forms of learning:

```text
Memory learning
remember a useful fact/context
→ Letta

Process learning
change how the task is performed
→ Git/process version

Capability learning
measurable improvement on task class
→ WorkerKit/evals

Economic learning
learn what resources/models/actions are worth spending on
→ LiveLLM
```

That decomposition will stop the Lab becoming vague.

For your own use, I would start with 3–4 workers whose outcomes are measurable and where there is real external demand:

```text
research-worker
coding/bounty-worker
hackathon-worker
opportunity-discovery-worker
```

Then perhaps later:

```text
SEO worker
customer-support worker
```

Do not start with 30 workers.

Each should have a clear ledger:

```text
LIFETIME

jobs attempted
jobs completed
jobs paid
revenue
LLM spend
tool/API spend
net
acceptance rate
human intervention
process versions
training cost
```

Then you can actually see whether “superagent” means anything.

The most interesting metric may be:

> **Return on worker improvement spend.**

Example:

```text
research-worker v8

School cost:
$4.20

Before:
72% success
$0.48 / accepted result

After:
88% success
$0.31 / accepted result

Subsequent production:
141 jobs

Value of improvement:
clearly positive
```

Now a “school” is economically real.

Likewise briefings:

```text
Dynamic briefing experiment
N=60

No briefing
success 68%
cost/outcome $0.81

Static briefing
success 74%
cost/outcome $0.73

History-derived briefing
success 86%
cost/outcome $0.49
```

Now you've discovered an actual product primitive.

And if the result is:

```text
all three ≈ same
```

you kill the feature.

That's exactly why using it yourself first is valuable.

The product roadmap I would use now is:

1. **Personal Lab**
2. Real workers
3. Real jobs
4. Versioned experiments
5. Real cost/revenue tracking
6. Learning/evaluation loops
7. Identify which internal artifacts repeatedly create value
8. Only then package those as:

   * schools
   * processes
   * briefings
   * evaluators
   * workers
   * leases
   * marketplace assets

That means the marketplace isn't speculative anymore.

It becomes:

> **things your own agents repeatedly proved were useful.**

There is also a strong startup advantage here: you become the first demanding user of your own infrastructure.

You will discover annoying realities like:

```text
briefings get stale
memory hurts performance
training overfits benchmark
model upgrades invalidate process
workers learn nonsense
cost accounting is wrong
retries destroy margins
Git history becomes noisy
capability scores drift
```

Those problems are exactly where the valuable product will emerge.

I would therefore narrow Moltwork's immediate mission to:

> **Build a private lab that can demonstrably turn generic agents into increasingly productive workers.**

Success criterion:

```text
Month 1 worker
vs
Month 3 worker

same task classes

higher success
lower cost
less supervision
more reusable capital
more revenue
```

If you can prove that on your own workers, the broader Moltwork market story becomes much stronger because you are no longer selling “agent careers” as a theory. You have actual workers with developmental histories and measured economic improvement.
